<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Burgraviate of Friedberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1216 CE – 1806 CE (approx.) · capital Friedberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Unusual corporate state of the castle knights of Friedberg, who elected their burgrave and became the leading power of the Wetterau.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedberg-hesse">Friedberg</a> castle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a> in the 12th century and garrisoned it with castle men, imperial knights who lived in the fortress. The burgrave, their commander, is documented from 1216. Over time the knights formed a self-governing corporation that elected the burgrave from their own ranks.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Burgraviate was governed by the burgrave and an assembly of castle knights, a kind of aristocratic republic. It acquired the Free Court of Kaichen and gradually took control over the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-friedberg">Free Imperial City of Friedberg</a>, which shared the hill. Its knights also led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a> imperial knights. Most members accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in the 16th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Burgraviate survived until the end of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, when it was mediatised and absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse</a> in 1806. The castle still dominates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedberg-hesse">Friedberg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/burgraviate-of-friedberg.jpg" alt="Burgraviate of Friedberg" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Haselburg-müller · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burg_Friedberg_Georgsbrunnen03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1216 CE – 1806 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Friedberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Imperial Burgraviate of Friedberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1511&amp;focus=burgraviate-of-friedberg" aria-label="Burgraviate of Friedberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4880 429 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Burgraviate of Friedberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5200 685L5229 694M5200 685L5198 698"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5198 698h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 685h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 685h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Friedberg. <a href="/atlas?year=1511&amp;focus=burgraviate-of-friedberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
